+# Build loading and unloading
+
+## The problem
+
+When a player with a large saved build joined, the server stalled for about 2,100 ms in one
+frame, almost all of it under `Script_Builds`. Every player rubber-banded, and clients then
+hitched in `Replicator ProcessPackets`. When a player with a large build left, there was a
+smaller freeze.
+
+The cause was in `src/ServerScriptService/Builds.server.luau`:
+
+- **The whole build was built in one frame.** `loadBuild` created every block in a single loop,
+  so a 20,000-block save meant 20,000 blocks in one server frame, and one large burst for every
+  client to process.
+- **Each block was three instances.** Every `Part` had an `Owner` `StringValue` and a `PlacedAt`
+  `NumberValue` under it, so a full build was 60,000 instances. Those value objects existed only
+  to hold server data, but each one was created, replicated to every client, and kept in memory
+  (about 600 bytes each).
+- **Parts were parented before their properties were set** (`Instance.new("Part", parent)`), so
+  each of the seven property writes was a change on a live, replicated instance.
+- **Unloading destroyed the whole plot folder at once**, which removed every block in one frame
+  on the server and on every client.
+
+## What changed
+
+All in `Builds.server.luau`.
+
+1. **Block data moved from value objects into a server-side table.** `blockRecords[part]` holds
+   `OwnerId`, `PlacedAt`, and `Cell` for each block. A build is now 20,000 instances instead of
+   60,000, and the 40,000 value objects (about 24 MB of instances per full build on the server,
+   plus their replication) are gone.
+   - The removal owner check now reads `blockRecords[block].OwnerId` instead of the `Owner`
+     value. Same rule: only the player who owns a block can remove it.
+   - Each block's placed time is kept in `blockRecords[part].PlacedAt`: the saved time for
+     loaded blocks, `os.time()` for newly placed ones, as before.
+2. **Parts are configured first and parented last**, so each one replicates once, fully set up.
+3. **Loading and unloading run across frames from one shared queue.** A `Heartbeat` handler
+   creates or destroys at most `BLOCKS_PER_FRAME` (200) blocks per server frame, shared by every
+   plot that is loading or unloading, working on plots in the order they were queued. A full
+   20,000-block build takes about 100 frames, about 1.7 seconds at 60 Hz.
+4. **Everything that must stay correct while a build is half loaded is done before the first
+   frame of work**:
+   - Every saved block's cell is reserved in `occupied` when loading starts (a table-only pass,
+     no instances). Another player cannot place a block into a cell the build is about to fill,
+     so a saved block is never skipped because of a race. Previously the same could not happen
+     because the build appeared all at once.
+   - If the player leaves mid-load, the remaining saved blocks are dropped and their cell
+     reservations released, and the blocks already created are unloaded.
+5. **Unloading** removes the owner's block records immediately (so the blocks no longer count as
+   anyone's, even if the player rejoins), then destroys the parts 200 per frame, freeing each
+   cell as its part is destroyed. The plot's slot is freed only after the last part is gone, so
+   a new player never loads into a plot that still holds the old build.
+6. **Players who join while every slot is taken now wait for a slot** instead of getting no plot
+   (previously: a `warn` and no plot for the rest of their session). With 8 players and 8 plots,
+   this happens when someone joins while a leaver's build is still unloading.
+
+Placing and removing blocks work as before: same remotes, same arguments, same validation (type
+and NaN checks, reach, rate limit, cell occupancy, owner check). A player can place blocks on
+their own plot while their build is still loading.
+
+## What players will notice
+
+- **Builds appear over about 1.7 seconds instead of all at once.** The test build fills layer
+  by layer from the ground up, because saved blocks are created in save order. Players cannot
+  place into a cell their build has not filled yet.
+- **When several players join at once, their builds load one after another.** With 8 full
+  builds joining together, the last one finishes after about 13 seconds. In normal play, joins
+  are spread out and each build takes under 2 seconds.
+- **A leaving player's build disappears over about 1.7 seconds** instead of vanishing at once.
+  No one can remove those blocks during that time.
+- **A player joining a full server while a build is unloading gets their plot when the
+  unload finishes**, up to about 1.7 seconds later (longer if loads are queued ahead of it).
+- **No more server-wide freeze or rubber-banding on join or leave**, and the client hitch in
+  `ProcessPackets` should shrink to a steady trickle of 200 parts per frame.
+
+## For other code
+
+- Block parts no longer have `Owner` or `PlacedAt` children. Nothing in this repo read them
+  outside `Builds.server.luau`, but any plugin, tool, or future script that did must read
+  `blockRecords` instead.
+- **The placed-time feature** should read `PlacedAt` from `blockRecords` on the server (move
+  the table into a module if another script needs it). If clients need placed times, send them
+  on demand for the blocks being looked at, or add a `PlacedAt` attribute. An attribute adds no
+  instance but does replicate with every part (about 24 bytes per block), so prefer sending on
+  demand for a whole-build view.
+- Saved block types that no longer exist, and saved cells that are already occupied, are still
+  skipped as before.
+- `SavedBuilds.load` may yield once it reads a DataStore: `openPlot` already handles a player
+  who leaves during that yield.
+
+## Tuning and verification
+
+These changes have **not been measured yet**: this work had no Studio session to profile. The
+numbers above are estimates from the block count and from benchmarks of the same patterns
+(setting properties then parenting 2,000 parts costs about 33 to 43 ms on a desktop, so 200
+parts is about 3.5 to 4.5 ms of a 16.7 ms frame).
+
+To verify, on the same workload as the original capture (a server with the 20,000-block test
+build, one player joining, then leaving):
+
+1. Take a server MicroProfiler capture across the join. Target: no frame over about 33 ms, and
+   `Script_Builds` under about 5 ms per frame while the build loads.
+2. Take a client capture on the joining player and on a player already in the server. Target:
+   `ProcessPackets` frames well under the previous hitch while the build streams in.
+3. Check `Stats.DataSendKbps` on the server during a load. If replication falls behind (parts
+   keep arriving on clients for long after the server finished), lower `BLOCKS_PER_FRAME`.
+   If server frames have room and the network keeps up, raise it to load faster.
+4. Repeat with several players joining together, and with a player leaving mid-load.
+5. Check on a phone that a joining client stays responsive while builds stream in.
